<Base title="About" description="About Talha M Blog: an independent news website explaining world, business, celebrity, sports, AI and Pakistan stories in simple English, run by founder and writer Talha Mehmood.">
  <section class="article-head"><h1>About</h1></section>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><em>Last updated: October 15, 2026</em></p>

    <p>Welcome to Talha M Blog. This is a news and information website where every story is explained in simple, clear English, so that anyone — a student, a shopkeeper, a busy office worker, or a reader whose first language is not English — can understand the news without struggling with hard words. News moves fast and is often written in a complicated way. We believe that understanding what is happening in the world should not depend on how strong your English is. That is the whole idea behind this website.</p>

    <p>Every post on Talha M Blog tries to answer a few simple questions honestly: What happened? When and where did it happen? Who is involved? And why does it matter to ordinary people? We write in our own words, in short clear paragraphs, and we try to give the background of a story as well as the latest update, so that a reader who is hearing about a topic for the very first time can still follow it from start to finish.</p>

    <h2>What we write about</h2>
    <p>Our stories are organised by their nature into categories, so you can read only what interests you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/category/ai/">AI</a>: new AI tools, AI companies and artificial intelligence news, explained simply for beginners.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/nature/">Nature</a>: remarkable places, animals, weather wonders and the natural world, explained step by step.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/business/">Business</a>: big companies, rich people, markets, jobs and money stories that affect ordinary life.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/conflict/">Conflict</a>: wars, attacks and tensions between countries, reported carefully and without sensation.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/sports/">Sports</a>: cricket, football and other sports news, records and stories.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/celebrity/">Celebrity</a>: news and life stories of famous people — actors, singers, athletes and public figures — including their background and family, written with respect.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/us-news/">US News</a>: important stories from the United States — big companies, famous cases, jobs, technology giants and events that affect the whole world.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/crime/">Crime</a>: major crime and court stories, written with care, where allegations are always described as allegations until a court decides.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/disasters/">Disasters</a>: earthquakes, floods, storms, accidents and rescue efforts around the world.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/pakistan-news/">Pakistan News</a>: politics, government decisions and important events inside Pakistan.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/awards/">Awards</a>: Nobel Prizes, film awards, honours and the people who win them.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/health/">Health</a>: health news and public health stories, explained plainly. These stories are information only, never medical advice.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/politics/">Politics</a>: governments, elections and big political decisions, explained without taking sides.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/technology/">Technology</a>: phones, apps, companies and the technology people use every day.</li>
      <li><a href="/category/space/">Space</a>: rockets, space stations, planets and the missions exploring them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Most of our stories are international, because our readers live in many countries. We also cover Pakistan, our home country. Wherever a story comes from, the rule is the same: it must be important, interesting or useful for ordinary readers.</p>

    <h2>How we prepare a story</h2>
    <p>Before a story is published, we check the facts against reliable news sources. For important stories, we compare reports from at least three independent sources, because a single report can be wrong, incomplete, or later corrected by the people involved. We write every post ourselves, in our own words. We do not copy and paste articles from other websites, and we do not publish rumours as if they were confirmed news. Where a report is not yet confirmed, we say so clearly inside the story, instead of hiding the doubt.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Every post shows the date and time it was published, in Pakistan time (PKT).</li>
      <li>When a story develops, we update the post and the page shows an "Updated" date.</li>
      <li>A green dot beside a post means it was published in the last 48 hours.</li>
      <li>Our stories name the sources they are based on, and our newer stories also link to the original reports, so you can read them yourself. We are adding links to older stories gradually.</li>
      <li>Like many modern publishers, we sometimes use AI writing tools to help prepare drafts. Every story is checked against reliable sources and edited before it is published, and the editor of this website is responsible for everything that appears here.</li>
      <li>The pictures on this website are illustrations made for our stories, so readers should not mistake them for real photographs of an event.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Ways to read and join in</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Comments:</b> Every story has a comments section where readers can share their views. You write your name and email to comment; your email address is never shown publicly. Please read the comment rules in our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>, and see the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> for how comment information is stored.</li>
      <li><b>Newsletter:</b> You can subscribe with your email address on any story page to receive our new stories by email. Subscribing is free, we ask you to confirm your email first, and every email carries an unsubscribe link. The newsletter is run through Kit, as explained in our Privacy Policy.</li>
      <li><b>Search:</b> Use the search page to find any story on the website by a name, a place or a topic.</li>
      <li><b>Install the app:</b> Talha M Blog can be installed on your phone or computer like an app, straight from your browser, so your favourite stories are one tap away. Installing is optional and free.</li>
      <li><b>Author page:</b> Our <a href="/author/">author page</a> introduces the writer and lists every story he has published.</li>
      <li><b>Light and dark modes:</b> You can switch the website between light and dark reading modes; your choice is remembered on your own device.</li>
      <li><b>Share buttons:</b> Every story can be shared to WhatsApp, Facebook, X and Telegram, or copied as a link, using the share buttons on the page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Our promise about honesty</h2>
    <p>Trust is the most valuable thing a news website has. It is earned slowly and lost quickly, so we follow some simple rules. We never invent quotations and put them in someone's mouth. We never make up numbers, statistics or earnings. If we do not know something, we say that we do not know it. If a famous claim turns out to be exaggerated — and this happens often with viral stories — we explain what is actually true, even when the truth is less exciting than the rumour. A smaller true story is worth more than a big false one.</p>

    <h2>Corrections and right of reply</h2>
    <p>News changes quickly, and even with careful checking, mistakes can happen. When we find a mistake — or when a reader finds one and tells us — we correct the post and mark it as updated, so readers can see that it changed. If a story is about you or your organisation and you believe something in it is wrong or unfair, you also have a right of reply: email us and, where it is fair, we will add your response to the story. Please email us at <a href="mailto:contact@talhablog.com">contact@talhablog.com</a> and tell us which post it is about. Genuine corrections are always welcome, and we are grateful to the readers who send them.</p>

    <h2>Who runs Talha M Blog?</h2>
    <p>Talha M Blog is run by <b>Talha Mehmood</b>, its founder and writer, an independent publisher from Pakistan. The website started in October 2026. This is a small, independent website — it is not part of any large media group, political party or company. Topics are chosen because they are important, interesting or useful for ordinary readers, never because someone paid for them to appear. You can read more about the writer, and see all of his stories, on the <a href="/author/">author page</a>.</p>

    <h2>Advertising and independence</h2>
    <p>The website is supported by advertising served by Google AdSense, which is described in our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>. Visitors from Europe and some US states are shown a consent message about advertising cookies, and every reader can manage their choices as explained in the Privacy Policy. Advertisements never decide what we write, and no advertiser can pay to change, hide or remove a story.</p>

    <h2>Why simple English?</h2>
    <p>Most of the world's people do not speak English as their first language, yet most of the world's news is published in English first. Students preparing for exams, young people looking for their first job, freelancers working with foreign clients, and millions of ordinary readers in Pakistan, India, Bangladesh, Africa and many other regions read the news in their second or third language. Writing simply is not writing less — it is writing with more care. If a story cannot be explained in simple words, it usually means the writer has not understood it fully himself.</p>

    <h2>Follow and contact us</h2>
    <p>You can follow Talha M Blog on Instagram at <a href="https://www.instagram.com/talh_ablog" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@talh_ablog</a>, where we share our newest stories. For questions, feedback, story suggestions or corrections, visit our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a> or email <a href="mailto:contact@talhablog.com">contact@talhablog.com</a>. We read every genuine message. Thank you for reading Talha M Blog — we hope you leave every story understanding a little more than when you arrived.</p>

